## Build

```sh
go mod tidy
go run github.com/hollis-labs/substrate/cmd/substrate pins sync --app .
go mod tidy
go build -o {{ .inputs.repo_name }} ./cmd/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}
./{{ .inputs.repo_name }} hello
```

## Deploy

`fleet-install deploy {{ .inputs.repo_name }} --commit <sha> --dry-run`, then without `--dry-run`. Set the host alias in `deploy.yaml` first.

## Installed configuration

The binary reads `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}/config.yaml` when present (fallback
`~/.config/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}/config.yaml`). `SUBSTRATE_CONFIG` explicitly selects another file.
Config discovery creates nothing and does not read from the checkout. Store credentials in an
operator-owned environment file or secret provider outside the repository.

{{ if .inputs.with_mcp }}
## MCP

This scaffold selects Substrate's agentic profile. Set `{{ .computed.env_prefix }}_TOKEN` through your
operator environment (or configure a Substrate verifier in the XDG config) before serving MCP.
The environment credential grants `{{ .inputs.repo_name }}.mcp` and `{{ .inputs.repo_name }}.read`;
no credential is rendered or stored by Folio. Help and print-only commands need no credentials.

`{{ .inputs.repo_name }} mcp --token-file <credential-file>` serves stdio. For an explicitly trusted
local caller, `mcp --local-trust` grants only endpoint/read scopes; it does not bypass a bad supplied
token or the per-tool scope check. The configured server verifier is still required. Local trust
is never read from config or used for HTTP. Add scopes deliberately when you add operations.
{{ if eq .inputs.tool_kind "service" }}
`serve` also exposes `/mcp` behind both the outer service guard and Substrate endpoint/tool scopes.
MCP-enabled services always require enforced auth, including development runs. The outer guard uses
the same Substrate verifier and scope table as the MCP endpoint and tool handlers.
{{ end }}
`ping` is a scoped read-only example. Replace its implementation with calls into the tool's service layer;
register every new tool's scope and call `mcp.Authorize` in its handler. Registry registration waits for
the catalog owner's agreed interface; this scaffold invents no registry endpoint.
{{ end }}
